ML_PAGE("l1-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l1\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 1 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain, apply to the scenario, and justify. The model answers below show the standard to aim for; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l1\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The 5-move answer formula (5-10 mark questions)</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Define</b><p>State the concept clearly - e.g. Mitchell's T-E-P.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Explain</b><p>How it works or why it matters.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Example</b><p>Ideally drawn from the scenario.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Contrast + apply</b><p>Compare with the nearest competing concept, then justify your choice for the scenario.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Most strong answers explain a relationship: problem → type of learning → how it learns → how we know it works.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box span-all\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p>\"Supervised learning because there are labels.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">Correct but thin: no mechanism, no task type, no link to the scenario.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"This is a supervised-learning problem because historical transactions already contain known target labels, namely Fraud and Legitimate. The algorithm can learn the relationship between transaction features and the known class during training. Since the output is one of two categories rather than a continuous value, the task is classification. After training, the model applies the learned relationship to unseen transactions.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-good\">Claim + because + task type + what happens next.</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"block-title\">Model-answer bank</h3>\n<div class=\"scenario-grid\">\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">DEFINE</span>\n        <h4>Define machine learning using T, E and P, with an example.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l1-1\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l1-1\">A system learns if its performance P on a task T improves through experience E. For a house-price model, T is predicting selling price, E is historical house records with their prices, and P is prediction error. If error on new houses falls after learning from past sales, the system has learned. Unlike traditional programming, where humans write the rules, ML derives the relationship from data and desired outcomes.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">COMPARE</span>\n        <h4>Compare overfitting and underfitting (symptoms, causes, remedies).</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l1-2\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l1-2\">Overfitting: the model performs well on training data but poorly on unseen data (e.g. 99% vs 65%), because it is too complex relative to the amount and noisiness of data and memorises noise. Remedies: simplify the model, gather more data, reduce noise. Underfitting: poor on both (e.g. 60% vs 58%) because the model is too simple to capture the structure. Remedies: a more powerful model, better features, fewer constraints. The goal is minimum error on unseen data, not on training data.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EVALUATE</span>\n        <h4>Evaluate KDD, CRISP-DM and SEMMA.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l1-3\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l1-3\">KDD turns raw data into knowledge through selection, preprocessing, transformation, data mining and interpretation. CRISP-DM is a business-driven, iterative cycle - business understanding, data understanding, preparation, modelling, evaluation and deployment - so it suits real organisational projects. SEMMA (sample, explore, modify, model, assess) concentrates on the analytics and model-building process. Their middle phases overlap; CRISP-DM is distinguished by starting from the business objective and ending with deployment.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Explain batch vs online learning with an application.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l1-4\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l1-4\">Batch learning trains on all available data, then stops learning once deployed; adapting requires retraining, as when a bank rebuilds its credit model monthly. Online learning updates incrementally as instances or mini-batches arrive, so it suits fast-changing problems such as fraud, where tactics evolve daily. Online methods also enable out-of-core learning on datasets too large for memory.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">CHALLENGES</span>\n        <h4>Explain why \"more data\" does not always produce a better model.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l1-5\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l1-5\">Quantity is only one requirement. Data must also be representative of the cases the model will face - 2 million records from one cardiac clinic will not generalise to the whole population - and of good quality, since errors, outliers and missing values hide real patterns. Features must also be relevant. Large data ≠ good data; relevant, representative and sufficient data is the goal.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">SCENARIO</span>\n        <h4>Classify a student pass/fail predictor along all three ML dimensions.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l1-6\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l1-6\">T = predict pass/fail, E = previous student records, P = prediction quality. Known outcomes make it supervised; a categorical output makes it classification. Retraining once per semester makes it batch learning; learning a general relationship rather than comparing to similar students makes it model-based. Check training vs test accuracy for over/underfitting, and organise the project with CRISP-DM.</div>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Practice questions and worked answers from the notes</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P1</span><span>How to write a subjective ML answer</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>For most 5-10 mark questions, use this structure:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Define the concept clearly.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Explain how it works or why it matters.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Give an example.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Contrast it with the nearest competing concept if relevant.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Apply it to the scenario and justify your choice.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>So if the question says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A bank has historical transactions labelled Fraud and Legitimate. Recommend an appropriate type of ML and explain.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A weak answer is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Supervised learning because there are labels.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A stronger answer is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This is a supervised-learning problem because historical transactions already contain known target labels, namely Fraud and Legitimate. The algorithm can therefore learn the relationship between transaction features and the known class during training. Since the required output is one of two categories rather than a continuous numerical value, the specific task is classification. After training, the model can apply the learned relationship to unseen transactions to predict whether each is fraudulent or legitimate.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is the standard you should aim for.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P2</span><span>A likely subjective question</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><blockquote>\n<p>A neural-network model achieves 99% accuracy on training data but only 68% on test data. Identify the problem and recommend solutions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A strong answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The model is likely overfitting because there is a large gap between training and test performance. It has learned the training observations very closely but does not generalize well to unseen cases. This can occur when model complexity is too high relative to the quantity or quality of training data. Possible remedies include reducing model complexity, obtaining more representative training data and reducing noise or erroneous observations. The goal is not simply to maximize training accuracy but to obtain a model that performs reliably on unseen data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's the kind of paragraph you want in an exam.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P3</span><span>A good 10-mark comparison answer</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><blockquote>\n<p>KDD, CRISP-DM and SEMMA are structured frameworks used to guide data-mining and machine-learning work, but they emphasize different parts of the process. KDD focuses on discovering useful knowledge from raw data through selection, preprocessing, transformation, data mining and interpretation. CRISP-DM has a broader project and business orientation because it begins with Business Understanding and Data Understanding before proceeding through Data Preparation, Modeling, Evaluation and Deployment. This makes it particularly suitable when the ML project must be aligned with organizational objectives and finally operationalized. SEMMA focuses more directly on the analytical workflow through Sample, Explore, Modify, Model and Assess. It emphasizes sampling, exploratory analysis, data transformation, modelling and performance assessment. Therefore, the three frameworks contain overlapping activities, but CRISP-DM provides the clearest end-to-end link between business objectives and deployment, while KDD emphasizes knowledge discovery and SEMMA emphasizes the analytical/model-building process.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's much better than dumping the acronyms.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P4</span><span>Scenario reasoning: how to identify the answer</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Suppose an exam gives:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A telecom company has labelled historical customer records showing whether each customer churned. Management wants to identify future customers who are likely to leave. The model is retrained every quarter.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Break it down.</p>\n<p>Known labels:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\Rightarrow \\text{Supervised}$$</div>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Churn / Not Churn\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\Rightarrow \\text{Classification}$$</div>\n<p>Retrained every quarter instead of updating continuously:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\Rightarrow \\text{Batch}$$</div>\n<p>If the algorithm learns a general relationship:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\Rightarrow \\text{Model}\\text{-}\\text{based}$$</div>\n<p>So one system can be:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Supervised}\n+\n\\text{Classification}\n+\n\\text{Batch}\n+\n\\text{Model}\\text{-}\\text{based}\n}$$</div>\n<p>This is exactly the sort of layered answer that demonstrates genuine understanding.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P5</span><span>Another subjective scenario</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><blockquote>\n<p>An e-commerce company does not know how many types of customers it has. It provides purchase histories to an algorithm, which automatically groups similar customers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You should say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This is unsupervised learning because the company has not supplied known customer-segment labels. More specifically, the task is clustering because the algorithm groups observations according to similarity and latent patterns within the data. The discovered clusters can subsequently be interpreted by the business, for example as high-value, discount-sensitive or occasional customers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Don't just write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Unsupervised.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Explain <em>why</em>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P6</span><span>Another scenario</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><blockquote>\n<p>A warehouse robot attempts different routes. Fast successful deliveries receive positive rewards while collisions receive penalties.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This is reinforcement learning because the robot learns by interacting with the environment and receiving reward or punishment from its actions. It is not given a correct route for every situation; instead, it must experiment and gradually learn a decision strategy that maximizes cumulative reward, such as delivering quickly while avoiding collisions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Definition} + \\text{evidence from scenario} + \\text{implication}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P7</span><span>What subjective questions I would prepare hardest</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Likely style</th>\n<th>What you should be able to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Define ML using T-E-P\"</td>\n<td>Explain all three and apply to example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Compare supervised, unsupervised, reinforcement\"</td>\n<td>Compare feedback + tasks + examples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Differentiate classification and regression\"</td>\n<td>Category vs continuous number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Batch vs online\"</td>\n<td>Explain update behaviour + application</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Instance vs model-based\"</td>\n<td>Similarity vs general relationship</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Explain challenges in ML\"</td>\n<td>Quantity, representativeness, quality, features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Compare overfitting and underfitting\"</td>\n<td>Symptoms, causes, remedies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Evaluate KDD, CRISP-DM, SEMMA\"</td>\n<td>Stages + emphasis + differences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scenario question</td>\n<td>Classify the problem and justify every choice</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr /></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l1\" id=\"l1s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">39 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Part 1 - How to write a subjective ML answer\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s1\" title=\"Part 1 - How to write a subjective ML answer\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Answer structure</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 2 - What is Machine Learning?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s2\" title=\"Part 2 - What is Machine Learning?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Defining ML</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 3 - Understand T, E and P properly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s3\" title=\"Part 3 - Understand T, E and P properly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>T, E and P</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 4 - What can ML actually do?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s4\" title=\"Part 4 - What can ML actually do?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>What ML can do</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 5 - The three different ways the lecture classifies ML\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s5\" title=\"Part 5 - The three different ways the lecture classifies ML\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Three ways to classify ML</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 6 - Supervised Learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s6\" title=\"Part 6 - Supervised Learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Supervised learning</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 7 - Unsupervised Learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s7\" title=\"Part 7 - Unsupervised Learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Unsupervised learning</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 8 - Reinforcement Learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s8\" title=\"Part 8 - Reinforcement Learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Reinforcement learning</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 9 - A strong three-way comparison\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s9\" title=\"Part 9 - A strong three-way comparison\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Comparing the three types</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 10 - Batch Learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s10\" title=\"Part 10 - Batch Learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Batch learning</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 11 - Online Learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s11\" title=\"Part 11 - Online Learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Online learning</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 12 - Batch vs Online subjective answer\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s12\" title=\"Part 12 - Batch vs Online subjective answer\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Model answer: batch vs online</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 13 - Out-of-core learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s13\" title=\"Part 13 - Out-of-core learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Out-of-core learning</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 14 - Instance-Based Learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s14\" title=\"Part 14 - Instance-Based Learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Instance-based learning</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 15 - Model-Based Learning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s15\" title=\"Part 15 - Model-Based Learning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Model-based learning</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 16 - Instance vs model-based: a good comparison\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s16\" title=\"Part 16 - Instance vs model-based: a good comparison\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Instance vs model-based</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 17 - Why machine learning fails\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s17\" title=\"Part 17 - Why machine learning fails\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Why ML fails</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 18 - Insufficient training data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s18\" title=\"Part 18 - Insufficient training data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Too little data</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 19 - Nonrepresentative training data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s19\" title=\"Part 19 - Nonrepresentative training data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>Nonrepresentative data</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 20 - Poor-quality data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s20\" title=\"Part 20 - Poor-quality data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Poor-quality data</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 21 - Feature engineering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s21\" title=\"Part 21 - Feature engineering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Feature engineering</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 22 - Overfitting\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s22\" title=\"Part 22 - Overfitting\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Overfitting</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 23 - Causes and fixes for overfitting\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s23\" title=\"Part 23 - Causes and fixes for overfitting\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Overfitting causes and fixes</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 24 - Underfitting\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s24\" title=\"Part 24 - Underfitting\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>Underfitting</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 25 - Overfitting vs underfitting\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s25\" title=\"Part 25 - Overfitting vs underfitting\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>Overfitting vs underfitting</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 26 - A likely subjective question\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s26\" title=\"Part 26 - A likely subjective question\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>Practice: 99% train, 68% test</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 27 - KDD, CRISP-DM and SEMMA\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s27\" title=\"Part 27 - KDD, CRISP-DM and SEMMA\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Frameworks overview</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 28 - KDD\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s28\" title=\"Part 28 - KDD\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>KDD</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 29 - CRISP-DM\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s29\" title=\"Part 29 - CRISP-DM\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>CRISP-DM</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 30 - Why CRISP-DM is important\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s30\" title=\"Part 30 - Why CRISP-DM is important\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>Why CRISP-DM matters</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 31 - SEMMA\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s31\" title=\"Part 31 - SEMMA\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>SEMMA</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 32 - KDD vs CRISP-DM vs SEMMA\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s32\" title=\"Part 32 - KDD vs CRISP-DM vs SEMMA\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>KDD vs CRISP-DM vs SEMMA</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 33 - A good 10-mark comparison answer\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s33\" title=\"Part 33 - A good 10-mark comparison answer\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>Model answer: frameworks (10 marks)</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 34 - Scenario reasoning: how to identify the answer\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s34\" title=\"Part 34 - Scenario reasoning: how to identify the answer\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>Scenario: telecom churn</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 35 - Another subjective scenario\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s35\" title=\"Part 35 - Another subjective scenario\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>Scenario: customer grouping</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 36 - Another scenario\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s36\" title=\"Part 36 - Another scenario\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Scenario: warehouse robot</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 37 - What subjective questions I would prepare hardest\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s37\" title=\"Part 37 - What subjective questions I would prepare hardest\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Likely questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 38 - The chapter as one connected story\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s38\" title=\"Part 38 - The chapter as one connected story\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Chapter as one story</button></li><li data-full=\"The answer-writing principle I want you to use\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1s-s39\" title=\"The answer-writing principle I want you to use\">Always say \"because\"</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l1\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Lecture 1 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>39 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>For a <strong>subjective exam</strong>, Lecture 1 becomes much easier if you stop treating it as a collection of definitions. Most good answers should explain a <strong>relationship</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"problem → type of learning → how it learns → how we know it works\"><span>problem</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>type of learning</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>how it learns</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>how we know it works</span></div>\n<p>The lecture itself is built around three outcomes: explaining what ML is, identifying the different ways ML can learn, and evaluating KDD, CRISP-DM and SEMMA. So those are the areas I would expect you to be able to <strong>explain, compare and apply</strong>, not merely recognize.</p><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 1 - How to write a subjective ML answer</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For most 5-10 mark questions, use this structure:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Define the concept clearly.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Explain how it works or why it matters.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Give an example.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Contrast it with the nearest competing concept if relevant.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Apply it to the scenario and justify your choice.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>So if the question says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A bank has historical transactions labelled Fraud and Legitimate. Recommend an appropriate type of ML and explain.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A weak answer is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Supervised learning because there are labels.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A stronger answer is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This is a supervised-learning problem because historical transactions already contain known target labels, namely Fraud and Legitimate. The algorithm can therefore learn the relationship between transaction features and the known class during training. Since the required output is one of two categories rather than a continuous numerical value, the specific task is classification. After training, the model can apply the learned relationship to unseen transactions to predict whether each is fraudulent or legitimate.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is the standard you should aim for.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 2 - What is Machine Learning?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_tradml\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Same goal, different author of the rules: in ML the algorithm produces them from data and desired outcomes.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture gives two useful definitions.</p>\n<p>Arthur Samuel's general idea is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A computer learns without every rule being explicitly programmed.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Tom Mitchell's engineering-oriented definition is more useful for a subjective answer:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Performance }P\n\\text{ on Task }T\n\\text{ improves through Experience }E\n}$$</div>\n<p>This <strong>T-E-P formulation</strong> is probably the best way to explain machine learning properly.</p>\n<p>Imagine a system predicting house prices.</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$T = \\text{predict selling price}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E = \\text{historical house records}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P = \\text{prediction accuracy/error}$$</div>\n<p>If the model becomes better at predicting house prices after learning from historical houses, it has learned.</p>\n<p>The key relationship is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E \\longrightarrow \\text{learning} \\longrightarrow P\\text{ improves on }T$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Traditional programming vs ML</h4>\n<p>Traditional programming:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Rules} + \\text{Data} \\rightarrow \\text{Output}$$</div>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">IF temperature &gt; 30\nTHEN turn fan ON\n</code></pre>\n<p>A human explicitly writes the rule.</p>\n<p>Machine learning:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Data} + \\text{Desired Outcome} \\rightarrow \\text{Learned Model}$$</div>\n<p>The algorithm tries to discover the useful relationship from examples.</p>\n<h5>Subjective question</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain the difference between traditional programming and machine learning.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h5>Good answer</h5>\n<p>Traditional programming requires a programmer to explicitly specify rules that map inputs to outputs. For example, a fan-control program may contain a rule stating that if temperature exceeds 30°C, the fan should switch on. In machine learning, the programmer instead supplies data and examples of desired outcomes, and the learning algorithm identifies useful patterns or relationships from the data. Therefore, traditional programming depends primarily on manually defined rules, whereas ML derives a model through experience with data.</p>\n<p>That is enough for most 4-5 mark questions.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 3 - Understand T, E and P properly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Don't just memorize the letters.</p>\n<h4>Task $T$</h4>\n<p>The task is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What exactly is the system supposed to do?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture says the task should be formulated as concretely as possible. A data sample normally contains attributes or <strong>features</strong> that the algorithm uses.</p>\n<p>Bad task definition:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Improve hospital operations.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Better:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Predict whether a patient will be readmitted within 30 days.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Even better because it is measurable.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Experience $E$</h4>\n<p>Experience is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>The data from which the model learns.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For a fraud model:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">historical transactions\n</code></pre>\n<p>For a house model:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">historical house sales\n</code></pre>\n<p>For image classification:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">images and labels\n</code></pre>\n<p>Experience may be supplied all at once or incrementally over time.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Performance $P$</h4>\n<p>Performance is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>The quantitative measure showing whether the model is doing the task well.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">prediction error\naccuracy\nprecision\nrecall\n</code></pre>\n<p>The exact metric depends on the task.</p>\n<p>A useful subjective sentence is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Without a suitable performance measure, we cannot determine whether additional experience has actually improved the model.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That shows understanding rather than memorization.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 4 - What can ML actually do?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Lecture 1 introduces several tasks, but the most important distinctions are classification, regression, clustering and anomaly detection.</p>\n<h4>Classification</h4>\n<p>Classification predicts a <strong>class or category</strong>.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Fraud / Legitimate\nPass / Fail\nSpam / Not Spam\nDisease / No Disease\n</code></pre>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X\\rightarrow \\text{Category}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture defines classification as assigning a specific class or category to each input sample.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Regression</h4>\n<p>Regression predicts a <strong>real numerical value</strong>.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">RM550,000 house price\n32.5°C temperature\nRM4,200 monthly sales\n</code></pre>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X\\rightarrow \\text{Number}$$</div>\n<p>The easiest exam distinction is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Category → classification.<br />\nQuantity → regression.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h4>Clustering</h4>\n<p>Clustering discovers natural groups based on similarities.</p>\n<p>Suppose a retailer has customer data but doesn't know what customer segments exist.</p>\n<p>The model might discover:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Cluster 1 → low spend, frequent buyer\nCluster 2 → premium buyer\nCluster 3 → occasional discount shopper\n</code></pre>\n<p>No predefined labels were given.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Anomaly detection</h4>\n<p>Anomaly detection looks for:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>observations that deviate from normal behaviour.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">usual card purchase = RM20-RM300\nsuddenly RM28,000 overseas at 3 AM\n</code></pre>\n<p>That transaction may be anomalous.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 5 - The three different ways the lecture classifies ML</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_types_tree\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Ask about the learning signal first, then about the shape of the target.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is an important subjective point.</p>\n<p>The lecture doesn't give one single classification.</p>\n<p>It classifies ML under <strong>three different questions</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Categories</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How much human supervision is involved?</td>\n<td>Supervised / Unsupervised / Reinforcement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Can it learn incrementally?</td>\n<td>Batch / Online</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How does it generalize?</td>\n<td>Instance-based / Model-based</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This means a model can simultaneously be:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Supervised}\n+\n\\text{Batch}\n+\n\\text{Model-based}$$</div>\n<p>They are <strong>not mutually exclusive categories from one list</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is a very good point to include in an essay answer because it demonstrates that you understand the structure of the chapter.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 6 - Supervised Learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Supervised learning uses <strong>labelled data</strong>. The model learns from input examples where the correct outcome is already known.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X + y$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X=\\text{features}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$y=\\text{known target}$$</div>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Temperature</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Vibration</th>\n<th>Failure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">72</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">1.3</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">105</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">8.7</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">81</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">2.2</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The model sees:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">inputs → known correct answer\n</code></pre>\n<p>and learns the relationship.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Classification and regression belong here</h4>\n<p>Classification:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X\\rightarrow \\text{category}$$</div>\n<p>Regression:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X\\rightarrow \\text{numerical value}$$</div>\n<p>So if an exam gives you:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Predict whether loan applicant defaults.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Supervised classification</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>because:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">known historical default labels\n+\ncategorical output\n</code></pre>\n<p>If it says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Predict amount of financial loss.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Supervised regression</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>because:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">known outcomes\n+\ncontinuous numerical target\n</code></pre>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 7 - Unsupervised Learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l1_clusters3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Unsupervised learning in 3D</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · Unlabelled customers in 3D (spend, visits, basket size). Run k-means and watch groups appear without any labels.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Unsupervised learning uses <strong>unlabelled data</strong> and searches for similarities or patterns rather than learning from known target labels.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$X$$</div>\n<p>rather than:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(X,y)$$</div>\n<p>Suppose you have customer information:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Income\nPurchase frequency\nAverage basket size\n</code></pre>\n<p>but there is no column saying:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Budget / Premium / Occasional\n</code></pre>\n<p>An unsupervised clustering algorithm can find groups based on similarity.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Supervised vs unsupervised - strong subjective answer</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Supervised</th>\n<th>Unsupervised</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Uses labelled data</td>\n<td>Uses unlabelled data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Known target $y$</td>\n<td>No known target</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Learns input → target relationship</td>\n<td>Finds underlying patterns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Classification/regression</td>\n<td>Commonly clustering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example: predict churn</td>\n<td>Example: discover customer segments</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A high-quality comparison sentence:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The fundamental distinction is not the algorithm name but whether the learning process has access to known target outcomes. Supervised learning learns against a known answer, while unsupervised learning attempts to discover latent structure without one.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 8 - Reinforcement Learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Reinforcement learning is about <strong>learning through interaction and consequences</strong>.</p>\n<p>The system takes an action and receives:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">reward\n</code></pre>\n<p>or:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">penalty\n</code></pre>\n<p>It gradually learns which behaviours produce better outcomes.</p>\n<p>The lecture uses the analogy of teaching a dog to fetch: successful fetching earns a treat, encouraging the desired behaviour. Its main objective is to maximize reward and minimize punishment.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"State → Action → Reward → Learning\"><span>State</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Action</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Reward</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Learning</span></div>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>A game-playing AI chooses:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">attack\nhide\nheal\nrun\n</code></pre>\n<p>and receives:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">+100 win\n-100 death\n+10 successful action\n</code></pre>\n<p>Over repeated interactions, it learns better decisions.</p>\n<h5>Exam note</h5>\n<p>Your slides describe reinforcement learning as \"also called semi-supervised.\" For a subjective answer, I would focus on the lecture's actual defining mechanism - <strong>experimentation, reward and punishment</strong> - rather than relying on that phrase.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 9 - A strong three-way comparison</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>If asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Compare supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Don't write three unrelated definitions.</p>\n<p>Show what differentiates them:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Supervised</th>\n<th>Unsupervised</th>\n<th>Reinforcement</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Learning signal</td>\n<td>Correct labels</td>\n<td>No labels</td>\n<td>Reward/penalty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Goal</td>\n<td>Predict target</td>\n<td>Discover structure</td>\n<td>Learn good actions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical task</td>\n<td>Classification/regression</td>\n<td>Clustering</td>\n<td>Decision-making</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td>Spam detection</td>\n<td>Customer segmentation</td>\n<td>Game-playing agent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Then conclude:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Therefore, the major difference lies in the feedback available to the learner. Supervised learning receives explicit target labels, unsupervised learning has no target labels, while reinforcement learning receives evaluative feedback from the consequences of actions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That final comparative sentence is often what separates a good answer from three copied definitions.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 10 - Batch Learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now we change the question.</p>\n<p>We are no longer asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Are labels available?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>We're asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>When does the system learn?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Batch learning trains using all available data, typically offline. Once deployed, it applies what it learned but does not continue updating until it is retrained.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">January-March data\n↓\nTrain\n↓\nDeploy model\n↓\nUse model during April\n↓\nRetrain again later\n</code></pre>\n<p>The model can still make predictions continuously.</p>\n<p>This is an important exam trap:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Real-time prediction does not mean online learning.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A batch-trained fraud model could make predictions in milliseconds while remaining completely unchanged internally.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 11 - Online Learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Online learning updates <strong>incrementally</strong>.</p>\n<p>New observations arrive:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Data}_1\\rightarrow \\text{Update}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Data}_2\\rightarrow \\text{Update}$$</div>\n<p>or small batches:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{MiniBatch}_1\\rightarrow \\text{Update}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture describes it as training sequentially using individual observations or mini-batches as new data arrives.</p>\n<p>Useful when:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">environment changes quickly\ndata arrives continuously\nfull retraining is expensive\n</code></pre>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>A fraud system might adapt to new fraud behaviour as transactions accumulate.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 12 - Batch vs Online subjective answer</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_batch_online\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Batch learns in big retraining events; online learns a little from every mini-batch.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose the exam asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Compare batch learning and online learning.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A strong answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Batch learning trains a model using the available training dataset as a whole and normally performs training offline. After deployment, the model applies its learned knowledge without updating until a new retraining cycle is performed. In contrast, online learning updates the model incrementally using individual new observations or small mini-batches. Consequently, batch learning is suitable when data patterns are relatively stable and periodic retraining is acceptable, whereas online learning is more suitable when data arrives continuously or changes rapidly. However, online learning also requires careful control because poor incoming data can immediately affect the model.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That last sentence is evaluation, not merely description.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 13 - Out-of-core learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Dataset}=2\\text{TB}$$</div>\n<p>but:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RAM}=16\\text{GB}$$</div>\n<p>You cannot load the entire dataset.</p>\n<p>An online algorithm can:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">load chunk 1\ntrain\ndiscard\nload chunk 2\ntrain\n...\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is <strong>out-of-core learning</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecture explicitly says online algorithms can be used for datasets too large to fit into main memory.</p>\n<p>Important:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Out-of-core does not necessarily mean continuously learning forever in production. It may simply use incremental learning because the training dataset is too large for memory.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 14 - Instance-Based Learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now we're asking another different question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How does the model generalize to a new case?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Instance-based learning essentially remembers examples and uses a <strong>similarity measure</strong> to judge new observations.</p>\n<p>Mental question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Which previous examples look like this?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Imagine a new customer:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age = 32\nIncome = RM90k\nSpending = RM3k\n</code></pre>\n<p>The system finds the most similar past customers and bases its prediction on them.</p>\n<p>A classic example would be nearest-neighbour-style reasoning.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 15 - Model-Based Learning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Model-based learning instead asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"What general relationship can I learn?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{HousePrice}\n=\n300(\\text{Size})\n+\n50000$$</div>\n<p>The system learns a general rule from the examples, then uses that rule for new cases.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Training Examples → Learned Model → Prediction\"><span>Training Examples</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Learned Model</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Prediction</span></div>\n<p>The slides contrast instance-based and model-based learning as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Which previous examples look like this?\" versus \"What general relationship can I learn from the examples?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 16 - Instance vs model-based: a good comparison</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l1_knn\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Instance-based versus model-based</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Instance-based prediction looks at the nearest stored examples; the model-based line predicts from a learned rule.</figcaption></figure></div><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Instance-based</th>\n<th>Model-based</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Relies strongly on stored examples</td>\n<td>Builds a general model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uses similarity</td>\n<td>Uses learned relationship</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"What looks like this?\"</td>\n<td>\"What rule explains this?\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New case compared to examples</td>\n<td>New case passed through model</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A good concluding sentence:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Instance-based learning generalizes locally by comparing a new case with similar examples, whereas model-based learning generalizes by estimating a broader relationship from the training data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 17 - Why machine learning fails</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is a very likely subjective topic because it lets the examiner ask you to <strong>evaluate</strong> an ML system.</p>\n<p>The lecture covers several data-related problems:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Too little data}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Nonrepresentative data}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Poor quality data}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Poor/irrelevant features}$$</div>\n<p>Then it covers model problems:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Overfitting}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Underfitting}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 18 - Insufficient training data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A model needs enough examples to identify reliable patterns.</p>\n<p>If you show someone:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">one dog\n</code></pre>\n<p>and ask them to identify every possible dog breed, that's rather ambitious.</p>\n<p>The same applies to ML.</p>\n<p>The lecture notes that complex applications such as image or speech recognition may require very large numbers of examples.</p>\n<p>The important subjective point:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Too little data means the model may learn accidental relationships rather than the true underlying pattern.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 19 - Nonrepresentative training data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is different from simply \"not enough data.\"</p>\n<p>Imagine you have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1,000,000\\ \\text{records}$$</div>\n<p>but all of them come from:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">young urban Malaysian customers\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you deploy the model to:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">the entire population\n</code></pre>\n<p>The dataset is huge.</p>\n<p>It is still potentially bad.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Training Population}\n\\neq\n\\text{Deployment Population}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture states that training data must represent the new cases to which the model will generalize.</p>\n<h5>Good subjective distinction</h5>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Insufficient data</strong> is mainly a quantity problem.<br />\n<strong>Nonrepresentative data</strong> is mainly a coverage/distribution problem.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is a useful exam sentence.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 20 - Poor-quality data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose your data contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Age = 250\nSalary = missing\nTemperature = &quot;banana&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>A model doesn't possess common sense that magically repairs this.</p>\n<p>Poor-quality data may contain:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">errors\noutliers\nnoise\nmissing values\ninconsistencies\n</code></pre>\n<p>And then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Garbage In}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Garbage Out}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture explicitly emphasizes this and links good performance to relevant features and reasonable data quality.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 21 - Feature engineering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture introduces feature engineering as producing a good set of inputs for the model.</p>\n<p>It gives three broad ideas:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature selection</td>\n<td>Keep the most useful existing features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature extraction</td>\n<td>Combine/transform existing information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Creating new features</td>\n<td>Gather or derive additional useful information</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>Original:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Debt\nIncome\n</code></pre>\n<p>Derived:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{DebtToIncome}=\n\\frac{\\text{Debt}}{\\text{Income}}$$</div>\n<p>That ratio may represent financial pressure better than either raw value alone.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 22 - Overfitting</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This one must be understood deeply.</p>\n<p>Overfitting means:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The model performs very well on the training data but fails to generalize to unseen data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TrainingAccuracy}=99\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TestAccuracy}=65\\%$$</div>\n<p>What happened?</p>\n<p>The model became too closely adapted to:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">training-specific details\nnoise\nexceptions\naccidental patterns\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of learning the general pattern.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>Imagine five dots:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">•    •\n  •\n       •\n    •\n</code></pre>\n<p>A sensible model captures the trend.</p>\n<p>An absurdly flexible model bends itself around every dot.</p>\n<p>That gives tiny training error.</p>\n<p>But the next point arrives somewhere new and the model performs terribly.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Low training error}\n\\neq\n\\text{Good model}\n}$$</div>\n<p>The real objective is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Good performance on unseen data}\n}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 23 - Causes and fixes for overfitting</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture says overfitting tends to occur when the model is too complex relative to the amount/noisiness of training data.</p>\n<p>Solutions include:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{simpler model}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{more training data}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{less noise} / \\text{fix errors} / \\text{remove problematic outliers}$$</div>\n<p>A strong answer should explain <em>why</em>.</p>\n<p>More training data makes it harder to memorize accidental patterns.</p>\n<p>A simpler model has less ability to fit every tiny irregularity.</p>\n<p>Reducing noise prevents the model from treating errors as useful structure.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 24 - Underfitting</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Underfitting is almost the opposite.</p>\n<p>The model is too simple to capture the true underlying relationship.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TrainingAccuracy}=58\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TestAccuracy}=56\\%$$</div>\n<p>Training is poor.</p>\n<p>Test is poor.</p>\n<p>The model never learned enough in the first place.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 25 - Overfitting vs underfitting</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l1_overfit\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Overfitting and underfitting</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Raise the model complexity and watch training error keep falling while test error rises again.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is worth memorizing conceptually:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Underfitting</th>\n<th>Good Fit</th>\n<th>Overfitting</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Model</td>\n<td>Too simple</td>\n<td>Appropriate</td>\n<td>Too complex/adapted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Training performance</td>\n<td>Poor</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Extremely good</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test performance</td>\n<td>Poor</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Poor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Generalization</td>\n<td>Poor</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Poor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fix</td>\n<td>More capable model/better features</td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Simpler model/more data</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture recommends more powerful models, better features and fewer constraints as ways to address underfitting.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 26 - A likely subjective question</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><blockquote>\n<p>A neural-network model achieves 99% accuracy on training data but only 68% on test data. Identify the problem and recommend solutions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A strong answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The model is likely overfitting because there is a large gap between training and test performance. It has learned the training observations very closely but does not generalize well to unseen cases. This can occur when model complexity is too high relative to the quantity or quality of training data. Possible remedies include reducing model complexity, obtaining more representative training data and reducing noise or erroneous observations. The goal is not simply to maximize training accuracy but to obtain a model that performs reliably on unseen data.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's the kind of paragraph you want in an exam.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 27 - KDD, CRISP-DM and SEMMA</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture identifies three major process frameworks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{KDD}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{CRISP}\\text{-}\\text{DM}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{SEMMA}$$</div>\n<p>Do <strong>not</strong> memorize three lists with no meaning.</p>\n<p>Give each framework a mental purpose.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 28 - KDD</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Raw Data}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Useful Knowledge}\n}$$</div>\n<p>The stages in the lecture are essentially:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Selection → Preprocessing → Transformation → Data Mining → Interpretation/Evaluation\"><span>Selection</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Preprocessing</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Transformation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Data Mining</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Interpretation/Evaluation</span></div>\n<h5>Selection</h5>\n<p>Choose and integrate the relevant data.</p>\n<h5>Preprocessing</h5>\n<p>Deal with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">missing values\nnoise\nerrors\ninconsistency\n</code></pre>\n<h5>Transformation</h5>\n<p>Convert data into a form suitable for mining and construct useful features.</p>\n<h5>Data Mining</h5>\n<p>Apply algorithms to discover patterns.</p>\n<h5>Interpretation/Evaluation</h5>\n<p>Interpret the discovered patterns so that they become understandable/useful knowledge.</p>\n<p>KDD therefore emphasizes the <strong>knowledge discovery process</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 29 - CRISP-DM</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>CRISP-DM is broader and starts from the actual organizational problem.</p>\n<p>The phases are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Business Understanding}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Data Understanding}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Data Preparation}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Modeling}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Evaluation}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Deployment}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture describes Data Preparation as cleaning and transforming data so that it is ready for model building.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Business Understanding</h4>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What problem are we actually solving?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>Bad:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Build an ML model.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Good:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Reduce customer churn by predicting which customers are likely to leave.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This stage translates business objectives into an ML/data-mining problem.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Data Understanding</h4>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">What data exists?\nIs it relevant?\nAre there quality problems?\nWhat initial patterns can we see?\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h4>Data Preparation</h4>\n<p>Deal with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">missing data\ntransformations\nfeature creation\nimportant features\ncleaning\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h4>Modeling</h4>\n<p>Choose and train suitable algorithms.</p>\n<p>Possibly compare multiple algorithms and tune them.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Evaluation</h4>\n<p>Ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Does the model actually satisfy the original objective?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A technically impressive model can still be useless if it solves the wrong problem.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Deployment</h4>\n<p>Put the model into practical use and make its output usable to the people who need it.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 30 - Why CRISP-DM is important</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose a manager says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Our factory has too much downtime.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You could rush to:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">RandomForestClassifier()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Congratulations, you've selected an algorithm without yet deciding what you're predicting.</p>\n<p>CRISP-DM forces you to first translate:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Reduce downtime\n</code></pre>\n<p>into something such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Predict whether a machine will fail within the next 24 hours.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Task}$$</div>\n<p>is clear.</p>\n<p>Then you can identify the required data and success criteria.</p>\n<p>That is why CRISP-DM is particularly good for explaining <strong>business-to-ML alignment</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 31 - SEMMA</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>SEMMA stands for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Sample → Explore → Modify → Model → Assess\"><span>Sample</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Explore</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Modify</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Model</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Assess</span></div>\n<p>The lecture explains the <strong>Explore</strong> stage as understanding variables individually and jointly, often using univariate/multivariate analysis and visualization.</p>\n<h5>Sample</h5>\n<p>Choose an appropriate subset of data.</p>\n<h5>Explore</h5>\n<p>Understand distributions, relationships and possible issues.</p>\n<h5>Modify</h5>\n<p>Clean, transform and engineer features.</p>\n<h5>Model</h5>\n<p>Apply modelling/data-mining methods.</p>\n<h5>Assess</h5>\n<p>Evaluate model performance and usefulness using data not used for training.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 32 - KDD vs CRISP-DM vs SEMMA</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_frameworks\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Highlighted bands show the steps the three frameworks share; CRISP-DM alone wraps them in business understanding and deployment.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is probably the most important framework comparison.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Framework</th>\n<th>Best mental interpretation</th>\n<th>Key emphasis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>KDD</td>\n<td>Data → knowledge</td>\n<td>Discovery from data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CRISP-DM</td>\n<td>Business problem → deployable solution</td>\n<td>Complete business/project lifecycle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SEMMA</td>\n<td>Data exploration → model assessment</td>\n<td>Analytical/model-building workflow</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>They overlap heavily.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">KDD Preprocessing\nCRISP-DM Data Preparation\nSEMMA Modify\n</code></pre>\n<p>are broadly solving related preparation problems.</p>\n<p>Similarly:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">KDD Data Mining\nCRISP-DM Modeling\nSEMMA Model\n</code></pre>\n<p>are related.</p>\n<p>So if asked to <strong>evaluate</strong> them, don't merely say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>They have different names.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Explain their emphasis.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 33 - A good 10-mark comparison answer</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><blockquote>\n<p>KDD, CRISP-DM and SEMMA are structured frameworks used to guide data-mining and machine-learning work, but they emphasize different parts of the process. KDD focuses on discovering useful knowledge from raw data through selection, preprocessing, transformation, data mining and interpretation. CRISP-DM has a broader project and business orientation because it begins with Business Understanding and Data Understanding before proceeding through Data Preparation, Modeling, Evaluation and Deployment. This makes it particularly suitable when the ML project must be aligned with organizational objectives and finally operationalized. SEMMA focuses more directly on the analytical workflow through Sample, Explore, Modify, Model and Assess. It emphasizes sampling, exploratory analysis, data transformation, modelling and performance assessment. Therefore, the three frameworks contain overlapping activities, but CRISP-DM provides the clearest end-to-end link between business objectives and deployment, while KDD emphasizes knowledge discovery and SEMMA emphasizes the analytical/model-building process.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's much better than dumping the acronyms.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 34 - Scenario reasoning: how to identify the answer</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose an exam gives:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A telecom company has labelled historical customer records showing whether each customer churned. Management wants to identify future customers who are likely to leave. The model is retrained every quarter.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Break it down.</p>\n<p>Known labels:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\Rightarrow \\text{Supervised}$$</div>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Churn / Not Churn\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\Rightarrow \\text{Classification}$$</div>\n<p>Retrained every quarter instead of updating continuously:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\Rightarrow \\text{Batch}$$</div>\n<p>If the algorithm learns a general relationship:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\Rightarrow \\text{Model}\\text{-}\\text{based}$$</div>\n<p>So one system can be:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Supervised}\n+\n\\text{Classification}\n+\n\\text{Batch}\n+\n\\text{Model}\\text{-}\\text{based}\n}$$</div>\n<p>This is exactly the sort of layered answer that demonstrates genuine understanding.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 35 - Another subjective scenario</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><blockquote>\n<p>An e-commerce company does not know how many types of customers it has. It provides purchase histories to an algorithm, which automatically groups similar customers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You should say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This is unsupervised learning because the company has not supplied known customer-segment labels. More specifically, the task is clustering because the algorithm groups observations according to similarity and latent patterns within the data. The discovered clusters can subsequently be interpreted by the business, for example as high-value, discount-sensitive or occasional customers.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Don't just write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Unsupervised.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Explain <em>why</em>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 36 - Another scenario</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><blockquote>\n<p>A warehouse robot attempts different routes. Fast successful deliveries receive positive rewards while collisions receive penalties.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This is reinforcement learning because the robot learns by interacting with the environment and receiving reward or punishment from its actions. It is not given a correct route for every situation; instead, it must experiment and gradually learn a decision strategy that maximizes cumulative reward, such as delivering quickly while avoiding collisions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Definition} + \\text{evidence from scenario} + \\text{implication}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 37 - What subjective questions I would prepare hardest</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Likely style</th>\n<th>What you should be able to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Define ML using T-E-P\"</td>\n<td>Explain all three and apply to example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Compare supervised, unsupervised, reinforcement\"</td>\n<td>Compare feedback + tasks + examples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Differentiate classification and regression\"</td>\n<td>Category vs continuous number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Batch vs online\"</td>\n<td>Explain update behaviour + application</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Instance vs model-based\"</td>\n<td>Similarity vs general relationship</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Explain challenges in ML\"</td>\n<td>Quantity, representativeness, quality, features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Compare overfitting and underfitting\"</td>\n<td>Symptoms, causes, remedies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Evaluate KDD, CRISP-DM, SEMMA\"</td>\n<td>Stages + emphasis + differences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scenario question</td>\n<td>Classify the problem and justify every choice</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 38 - The chapter as one connected story</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is what I would mentally reconstruct in the exam hall.</p>\n<p>We start with a <strong>real-world task</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$T$$</div>\n<p>The model gains:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E=\\text{experience from data}$$</div>\n<p>and we measure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P=\\text{performance}$$</div>\n<p>If labels exist:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Supervised}$$</div>\n<p>If no labels and we search for structure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Unsupervised}$$</div>\n<p>If behaviour is learned through rewards:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Reinforcement}$$</div>\n<p>Then ask how learning occurs over time:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Batch}\\quad \\text{vs}\\quad \\text{Online}$$</div>\n<p>Then ask how the system generalizes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Instance}\\text{-}\\text{based}\\quad \\text{vs}\\quad \\text{Model}\\text{-}\\text{based}$$</div>\n<p>Regardless of algorithm, success depends on:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Enough Data}\n+\n\\text{Representative Data}\n+\n\\text{Good Quality}\n+\n\\text{Useful Features}$$</div>\n<p>Then evaluate generalization:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Too simple}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{Underfitting}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Too adapted to training}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{Overfitting}$$</div>\n<p>Finally, use a process framework:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{KDD},\\ \\text{CRISP}\\text{-}\\text{DM},\\ \\text{or SEMMA}$$</div>\n<p>to structure the larger ML project.</p>\n<p>That is <strong>Lecture 1 as one logical system</strong>, rather than 56 slides to memorize.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1s-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">The answer-writing principle I want you to use</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For subjective questions, don't stop at:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"It is supervised learning.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"It is supervised learning <strong>because</strong> labelled target outcomes are available.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Don't stop at:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"The model is overfitting.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"The model is overfitting <strong>because</strong> training performance is very high while unseen/test performance is substantially worse, indicating poor generalization.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Don't stop at:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Use CRISP-DM.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"CRISP-DM is suitable <strong>because</strong> the problem begins with a business objective that must be translated into a machine-learning task, followed by data understanding, preparation, modelling, evaluation and eventual deployment.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For a subjective paper, <strong>the word \"because\" is practically free marks</strong>: claim + justification beats a naked definition almost every time.</p></div></details></div></details>");
